# Theorem 12 (`thm-mingens`) for the actual `w k`

`MinimalGenerators.lean` proves the general statement — independent same-degree
generators are minimal — for an abstract family `g`.  `Independence.lean` proves
the two hypotheses it needs for the real object: `isHomogeneous_w'` and
`w_linearIndependent`.

This file does nothing but compose them, and it exists precisely so that the
composition is a *checked declaration in the repository* rather than a claim in
a report.  The two halves living in separate files is exactly the situation in
which "and therefore the theorem follows" goes unverified for a long time.

It is a separate module rather than an addition to `Independence.lean` so that
`Independence` need not import `MinimalGenerators`; the two are independent of
each other and it is worth keeping them so.
-/
import KCommuting.MinimalGenerators
import KCommuting.Independence

open MvPolynomial

namespace KCommuting

variable {F : Type*} [Field F]

/-- **Theorem 12 (`thm-mingens`), for the real `w k`.**

For every `k ≥ 2`, no component of `w k` lies in the ideal generated by the
other two — i.e. the three components are a *minimal* generating set of `I_k`.

Both hypotheses of `three_generators_minimal` are discharged here for the actual
recursion, not assumed: homogeneity of degree `k` by `isHomogeneous_w'`, and
`F`-linear independence by `w_linearIndependent`. -/
theorem w_minimal_generators {k : ℕ} (hk : 2 ≤ k) (i : Fin 3) :
    (w (F := F) k) i ∉ Ideal.span ((w (F := F) k) '' {i}ᶜ) :=
  three_generators_minimal k (w (F := F) k) (fun j => isHomogeneous_w' hk j)
    (w_linearIndependent hk) i

end KCommuting
